00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:39.810
Part 4: the decoupling of wages and productivity.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.060
That's what people really want to hear about
in bonus videos.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.340
So Hank, in your last video you were like:

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:46.619
Hank: And I also worry about the decoupling
of productivity and wages.

00:01:46.619 --> 00:01:49.929
John: As it happens, that's something that
I also spend a lot of time thinking about.

00:01:49.929 --> 00:01:54.840
So as you can tell from this graph, since
World War II wages and productivity were really

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.060
closely correlated until about 2000, when
there was this great decoupling.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.439
And ever since then productivity has surged
but wages have remained stagnant.

00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:06.249
This is a big part of why we've seen this
dramatic increase in the disparity of wealth

00:02:06.249 --> 00:02:08.090
in the United States in the last, like 20
years.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:13.120
These days the richest 1% of Americans control
about 40% of America's total wealth.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:14.120
And that's bad.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:18.040
Not just morally bad, it's also economically
and socially bad.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:22.239
Decreasing economic mobility means that, on
average, the most talented and innovative

00:02:22.239 --> 00:02:27.170
people are less likely to get the opportunities
they need to create new businesses and make

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:28.850
lots of jobs, and etc.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:31.060
But anyway Hank, that graph only goes back
to 1947.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:36.409
If you go back much further, say to the industrial
revolution, you see a similar decoupling between

00:02:36.409 --> 00:02:41.120
productivity and wages which this economist
Robert Allen calls the Engel's Pause.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.210
And the Engel's Pause lasted several decades
until wages suddenly shot up, and it's possible

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:48.660
I guess that we're in the middle of another
Engel's Pause, because we are having this,

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.510
like, technological revolution, right?

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.219
But it's also possible that something entirely
different is happening because the sample

00:02:53.219 --> 00:02:55.049
size of history is tiny!

00:02:55.049 --> 00:03:00.530
Like, Hank, for almost all of the last 10,000
years, pretty much everyone's job was either

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:02.550
farmer or hunter/gatherer.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:06.011
It's only in like the last 300 years that
lots and lots of people have even had these

00:03:06.011 --> 00:03:08.690
non-farm jobs that people associate with 'wages.'

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:12.329
And that lack of historical precedence is
a big part of the reason I feel skeptical

00:03:12.329 --> 00:03:17.430
towards pretty much anyone who expresses confidence
when it comes to fiscal policy or economic

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:18.430
history.

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:21.219
In short, Hank, I think wealth inequality
is one of the biggest issues facing the United

00:03:21.219 --> 00:03:24.799
States and I think we should be very worried
about the decoupling of wages and productivity,

00:03:24.799 --> 00:03:29.370
but we also need to remember that we are in
the middle of history and we can collectively

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:33.280
decide whether this decreasing economic mobility
is permanent.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:34.469
Of course, I might be wrong.

00:03:34.469 --> 00:03:37.170
The possibility that you might be wrong is
the very definition of economics.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.769
Hank, nerdfighters, I'd love to hear more
of your thoughts on this.

00:03:39.769 --> 00:03:43.670
In the mean time don't forget to vote for
your state at papertownsmovie.com/tour, link

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:44.670
in the dooblydoo.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.170
And I hope to see as many of you as possible
next week in Europe.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:48.489
Hank, you will see me tomorrow.

